9. Add local Harbor MCP server surface for agents
   Scope: expose local Harbor itself as an inbound local MCP server that agents can connect to after plugins are installed through the UI. This may be implemented inside `apps/harbor-local` as an MCP endpoint or a small companion command/server, but it must call the same SDK/local API backend rather than duplicating OAuth, credential storage, discovery, indexing, search, or invocation logic.
   Output: users can connect local Harbor to Codex, Claude, or another MCP-capable agent and use tools such as catalog/source listing, source refresh, tool search, schema lookup, tool invocation, and invocation history. The MCP surface should expose registry search/invoke control-plane tools first, not generate one static MCP tool for every connected plugin tool.
   Progress: pending
   Commit: after this step is complete, commit MCP server code, connection docs, and focused tests.

10. Add local diagnostics and invocation history
   Scope: show local runtime state without exposing token values. Persist invocation records in SQLite with normalized metadata and raw MCP output. Include source health, OAuth status, discovery status, SQLite path, credential vault status, and actionable errors.
   Output: users can understand what local Harbor is doing and debug MCP failures without reading internal files.
   Progress: pending
   Commit: after this step is complete, commit diagnostics/history changes and tests.

11. Validate fresh local project and MCP flows
   Scope: run focused typechecks, tests, and manual/local smoke flows from a fresh `.harbor/` directory. Validate no-auth fixture MCP, OAuth-capable MCP where credentials are available, automatic discovery after connect, search, schema lookup, invocation, persisted history, restart durability, and local Harbor MCP agent connection.
   Output: local Harbor MCP console and local Harbor MCP server work from a clean local project, and failures are documented with exact blockers.
